  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 320 AM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows troughing continuing to sink \nsoutheastward over northern MN and Lake Superior. The parent low \npressure center associated with this trough will move in over the \nHudson Bay region leaving the upper-jet locally over the northern \nplains. This will help develop scattered mid to high clouds \nthrough the course of today. Given the passage of this system, \nit is expected to be another breezy day with wind gusts, \nespecially north and east of I-94 could range between 20-25 mph \nlater this morning into the afternoon. Temperatures will not be \nas warm as they were yesterday. Given our winds have shifted \nNW'ly cooler air should have no issue mixing towards the \nsurface. Central MN can anticipate highs in the upper 60s while \nsouthern MN see the lower 70s. As the forecast remains dry, \nwarmer temps combined with breezy conditions will support \ncontinued elevated fire weather conditions, especially across \nportions of western MN. \n\nCome Thursday, the apex of the upper-trough slides east over the \neastern Great Lakes region. Upper-ridging accompanied with building \n1020mb surface high pressure will keep the forecast quiet for the \nrest of the week. Thursday's highs will be closer to seasonal values \n(mid-60s) but are expected to rebound quickly for the weekend. The \nforecast remains quiet but an only exception is that guidance was \ntrying to generate a weak disturbance Friday afternoon across the \nwest-central plains and perhaps produce some weak rain showers \nacross southern MN. However, latest NBM PoPs have better confidence \nfor seeing rainfall is within Iowa. For Saturday, H850 temperature \nanomalies are forecasted (+12-16 deg) to be similar to what we saw \nyesterday. Currently the NBM is forecast to warm highs into the \nlower 80s for most areas Saturday but given the warmer \nverification from obs on Tuesday, its possible that Saturday's \nhighs may be needed to nudge warmer. Additionally, increased \nwinds between 30-40 mph will be possible as a surface warm front\nsweeps through. This will create another day of elevated fire \nweather concerns. Rainfall chances remain in place for early \nnext week as another low pressure system move across the\nnorthernplains. As mentioned in the previous discussion, total\nQPF appears to be decreasing. Temps will return to the upper \n50s and lower 60s.",
